[[:db/retract 17592186152476 :question/answer-fields 17592186149410] [:db/retract 17592186152476 :question/answer-fields 17592186149405] {:db/id 17592186152055 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-44155916-7397-5b39-a575-6db603bf8071" "question-025be531-fd73-5fa9-9ce4-f3e4c0c054c5" "question-ab21706d-8653-5637-8475-77432c4bfb1b" "question-da9aaccf-ed58-5942-8a55-f387c4ff158b" "question-7119d9e9-8c81-54dd-a944-533c5aa1aed1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151837 :question/problem "What is $26 \\textrm{ mod } 3?$" :question/worked-solution "The residue of $26$ modulo $3$ is the integer $0 \\leq r <  3$ such that\n\n$$\n26 \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,3).\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r,$ we need to write $26$ as an integer multiple of $3,$ plus the residue $r.$\n\nWe note that $26 \\equiv 2 \\, (\\textrm{mod}\\,3)$ because\n\n$$\n26 = 8\\cdot 3 + {\\color{blue}{2}} .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $26$ modulo $3$ is ${\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the subtraction property. Remember that any integer multiple of $3$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}26 & ≡26-0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\  & ≡26-8\\cdot 3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\  & ≡26-24 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\  & ≡2 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $26$ modulo $3$ is ${\\color{blue}{2}}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152056 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-549f3a8c-907a-5b2d-8f07-7849e3e239ab" "question-0a2135af-f11d-5278-a560-c7cd73d93e86" "question-24b40dbb-8c36-5294-b359-e391ac1e418a" "question-9feebe3a-396b-5d25-94bd-022707c998e2" "question-6d645eda-2214-5faa-a63b-2fee859e4238"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151838 :question/problem "What is $-13 \\textrm{ mod } 4?$" :question/worked-solution "The residue of $-13$ modulo $4$ is the integer $0 \\leq r < 4$ such that\n\n$$\n-13 \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,4).\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r,$ we need to write $-13$ as an integer multiple of $4,$ plus the residue $r.$\n\nWe note that $-13 \\equiv 3 \\, (\\textrm{mod}\\,4)$ because\n\n$$\n-13 = (-4)\\cdot 4 + {\\color{blue}{3}} .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-13$ modulo $4$ is ${\\color{blue}{3}}.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the addition property. Remember that any integer multiple of $4$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-13 & ≡-13+0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡-13+4\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡-13+16 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $-13$ modulo $4$ is ${\\color{blue}{3}}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152057 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-39ab88df-75d5-5304-96dd-f494bc2a5583" "question-e4503f6b-114f-5eeb-b12d-95fc38bf1562" "question-c3a0e9ee-2041-5135-bc63-3a864333d5da" "question-2ee231c9-5634-53c5-9930-57fd0fc0e5d4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151857 :question/problem "Let $a$ be a positive integer. Construct an expression that gives the last digit of $24a+15.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $b=24a+15.$ Note that $b$ is positive. Now, let $d_3,$ $d_2,$ and $d_1$ be the last three digits of the number $b{:}$ \n\n$$\nb = \\ldots d_3 d_2 d_1\n$$\n\nThen, $b$ can be written in expanded form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =⋯+100{d}_{3}+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} \\\\  & =10(⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the residue of $b$ modulo $10{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ≡10(⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0\\cdot (⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $d_1$ is the last digit of $b.$\n\nFinally, since $b = 24a+15,$ and \n\n$$\n24\\equiv 4, \\qquad 15\\equiv 5 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,10)\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ 24a+15 & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ 4a+5 & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression that gives the last digit of $24a+15$ is the following:\n\n$$\n(4a+5) \\: \\textrm{mod}\\: 10\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-44155916-7397-5b39-a575-6db603bf8071" :question/id #uuid "44155916-7397-5b39-a575-6db603bf8071" :question/math-academy-id "q-102405" :question/problem "$39\\text{ mod }4=$" :question/worked-solution "The residue of $39$ modulo $4$ is the integer $0\\le r<4$ such that\n\n$$\n39≡r\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4).\n$$\n\n**Method 1**\n\nTo find the residue $r,$ we need to write $39$ as an integer multiple of $4,$ plus the residue $r.$\n\nWe note that $39≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$ because\n\n$$\n39=9\\cdot 4+3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $39$ modulo $4$ is $3.$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can use the subtraction property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}39 & ≡39-0 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡39-9\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡39-36 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\  & ≡3 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residue of $39$ modulo $4$ is $3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8c08d870-0669-5e1c-afb0-29f7f768fa70"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8c08d870-0669-5e1c-afb0-29f7f768fa70" :answer-field/id #uuid "8c08d870-0669-5e1c-afb0-29f7f768fa70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8b0f9a90-f1d4-5ec0-95b4-48df57d2cbe6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d6be7669-e478-5e84-a892-5c0f1ec270f8" "answer-e8a6f499-512e-573b-8f2a-505f318cb0fc" "answer-8b0f9a90-f1d4-5ec0-95b4-48df57d2cbe6" "answer-3339da0a-421f-5923-8220-0d40e5fd564c" "answer-a11bb931-7d3d-55b5-b9c9-3767270b03bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6be7669-e478-5e84-a892-5c0f1ec270f8" :answer/id #uuid "d6be7669-e478-5