c9-5634-53c5-9930-57fd0fc0e5d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-212918" :question/problem "Let $a$ be a postive integer. Construct an expression that gives the last digit of $12a+11.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $b=12a+11.$ Note that $b$ is positive. Now, let ${d}_{3},$ ${d}_{2},$ and ${d}_{1}$ be the last three digits of the number $b:$ \n\n$$\nb=…{d}_{3}{d}_{2}{d}_{1}\n$$\n\nThen, $b$ can be written in expanded form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =⋯+100{d}_{3}+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} \\\\  & =10(⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the residue of $b$ modulo $10:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ≡10(⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0\\cdot (⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${d}_{1}$ is the last digit of $b.$\n\nFinally, since $b=12a+11,$ and \n\n$$\n12≡2,\\,11≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)\n$$\n\nwe have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ 12a+11 & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ 2a+1 & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression that gives the last digit of $12a+11$ is the following:\n\n$$\n2a+1\\,\\text{mod}\\,10\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1c808650-670f-5359-b03d-37e5210f7aab" "answer-field-97815a78-cd71-53e4-88a2-4dcae4f9772e" "answer-field-3be7a443-2e51-5bc0-813e-d118e2293cf7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1c808650-670f-5359-b03d-37e5210f7aab" :answer-field/id #uuid "1c808650-670f-5359-b03d-37e5210f7aab" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-fc37fd48-8db7-549b-9bca-a9e299c9ef5d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cf382bf7-5ade-5948-8966-a05213deac20" "answer-fc37fd48-8db7-549b-9bca-a9e299c9ef5d" "answer-0179ab06-b666-5897-8b46-ee9334267d51" "answer-e614fd17-a49f-57f4-9885-3e191a459935"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf382bf7-5ade-5948-8966-a05213deac20" :answer/id #uuid "cf382bf7-5ade-5948-8966-a05213deac20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2a" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc37fd48-8db7-549b-9bca-a9e299c9ef5d" :answer/id #uuid "fc37fd48-8db7-549b-9bca-a9e299c9ef5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2a+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0179ab06-b666-5897-8b46-ee9334267d51" :answer/id #uuid "0179ab06-b666-5897-8b46-ee9334267d51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e614fd17-a49f-57f4-9885-3e191a459935" :answer/id #uuid "e614fd17-a49f-57f4-9885-3e191a459935" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-97815a78-cd71-53e4-88a2-4dcae4f9772e" :answer-field/id #uuid "97815a78-cd71-53e4-88a2-4dcae4f9772e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-daeddf0a-57e1-54cb-8951-4d9c6add6d18" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d198daac-e17a-5abf-8411-da0f88a260a5" "answer-e30c4148-f70f-5d0a-9080-6b7d1032ff53" "answer-daeddf0a-57e1-54cb-8951-4d9c6add6d18"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d198daac-e17a-5abf-8411-da0f88a260a5" :answer/id #uuid "d198daac-e17a-5abf-8411-da0f88a260a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\,\\,\\times " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e30c4148-f70f-5d0a-9080-6b7d1032ff53" :answer/id #uuid "e30c4148-f70f-5d0a-9080-6b7d1032ff53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\,\\,÷" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-daeddf0a-57e1-54cb-8951-4d9c6add6d18" :answer/id #uuid "daeddf0a-57e1-54cb-8951-4d9c6add6d18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{mod}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3be7a443-2e51-5bc0-813e-d118e2293cf7" :answer-field/id #uuid "3be7a443-2e51-5bc0-813e-d118e2293cf7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-23664f96-879c-51bd-89f0-dede9cc02253" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ea0b7f8d-2256-53e2-9eef-aac855a90178" "answer-23664f96-879c-51bd-89f0-dede9cc02253" "answer-2e60a1c1-361a-59b7-a94d-06e92eeefa00" "answer-5236d587-b4f8-5df5-b8f4-ecdf8d9f02eb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea0b7f8d-2256-53e2-9eef-aac855a90178" :answer/id #uuid "ea0b7f8d-2256-53e2-9eef-aac855a90178" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23664f96-879c-51bd-89f0-dede9cc02253" :answer/id #uuid "23664f96-879c-51bd-89f0-dede9cc02253" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e60a1c1-361a-59b7-a94d-06e92eeefa00" :answer/id #uuid "2e60a1c1-361a-59b7-a94d-06e92eeefa00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "100" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5236d587-b4f8-5df5-b8f4-ecdf8d9f02eb" :answer/id #uuid "5236d587-b4f8-5df5-b8f4-ecdf8d9f02eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153448 :tutorial/content "The **residue** of an integer $a$ modulo $n$ is the *unique* integer $0 \\leq r <  n$ such that\n\n$$\na \\equiv r \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nThis integer $r$ is sometimes called the **least residue** of $a$ modulo $n.$ \n\nFor example, consider the following statements:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25 & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ 25 & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ 25 & ≡13\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ 25 & ≡19\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of these statements are true. However, we say that \"the residue of $25$ modulo $6$ is ${\\color{blue}{1}}$\", because $0\\leq {\\color{blue}{1}} < 6.$ We write this as\n\n$$\n25 \\textrm{ mod } 6 = 1.\n$$\n\nNotice that there are no parentheses around the $\\textrm{mod}$. When there are no parentheses, it means that we're treating $\\textrm{mod}$ as though it were a type of function. This function takes our integer $25$ as its input and returns the remainder that we get when $25$ is divided by $6.$ \n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\text{ mod }6 & =1 \\\\ 13\\text{ mod }6 & =1 \\\\ 19\\text{ mod }6 & =1 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll present two methods of computing residues. Make sure that you understand *both* methods." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153464 :tutorial/content "Some interesting problems in number theory require finding the last few digits of a large integer. We can use properties of congruences to help us solve these types of problems.  \n\nConsider a positive number $a,$ where the digit $d_1$ is in the ones place, $d_2$ is in the tens place, $d_3$ is in the hundreds place, and so on.\n\n$$\na = \\ldots d_3 d_2 d_1\n$$\n\nThen, $a$ can be written in expanded form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =⋯+100{d}_{3}+10{d}_{2}+{d}_{1} \\\\  & =10(⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the residue of $a$ modulo $10{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & ≡10(⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0\\cdot (⋯+10{d}_{3}+{d}_{2})+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡{d}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression $a \\: \\textrm{mod}\\: 10$ returns the last digit of $a$.\n\nFor example, \n\n$$\n25\\,409\\,19{\\color{blue}3} \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: 10 = {\\color{blue}3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing similar reasoning, \n\n- the expression $a \\: \\textrm{mod}\\: 100$ returns the last two digits of $a,$ e.g., \n\n$$\n25\\,409\\,1{\\color{blue}93} \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: 100 = {\\color{blue}93}.\n$$\n\n- the expression $a \\: \\textrm{mod}\\: 1\\,000$ returns the last three digits of $a,$ e.g.,\n\n$$\n25\\,409\\,{\\color{blue}193} \\: \\textrm{mod} \\: 1\\,000 = {\\color{blue}193},\n$$\n\nand so on." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152370 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
